\clicktrack turns a click track on and off. It is off by
default. Its syntax is the following:

\clicktrack on
\clicktrack off

The click track clicks on the beats of the bar that plays,
as these are set with \beatat (or with \tempo), the same
beats the beat visualization pulses on. For example, with
\beatat 4. 4 in a bar of 5/8, it clicks on the first and
on the fourth eighth of the bar.
It clicks on the beats of a countdown too.

Each click is a short triangle wave at 440Hz, and the click
of the first beat of a bar is an octave higher, at 880Hz.
To change the frequency of the clicks, call its tune method
with a frequency in Hz:

\clicktrack.tune 442

This plays the clicks at 442Hz, and the first beat at
884Hz. The frequency can be a decimal number, and must be
greater than 0.

To change the volume of the clicks, call its volume method
with a value from 0 to 1. The default is 0.5:

\clicktrack.volume 0.3

The click track plays through the audio output of LiveLily,
together with Python (pyo), if it runs. The audio settings
(e.g. \samplerate and \buffersize) apply to it too, if they
are set before the click track or Python starts.
It is in time with the score and the instruments without a
delay (see the delay method in the help of \insts).
